WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.836
-Our next guest
is a writer, director,

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:04.570
and an Emmy Award-nominated
actor you know

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.673
from his work in shows
such as "Escape at Dannemora"

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:09.141
and films like "The Batman"
and "The Fabelmans."

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:10.810
He stars in "Dumb Money,"

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.245
which is in theaters
and on demand now.

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:13.947
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:16.082
-Hey, do you -- do you
remember back at Stonehill

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.517
when they dared me
to run that mile naked?

00:00:18.517 --> 00:00:20.420
-Yeah, there was
a crazy storm that night.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.955
-Everybody remembers that.
-Okay.

00:00:21.955 --> 00:00:23.222
-They do, man.
You don't think people

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:24.791
remember your 4:03 mile?

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:26.826
-Oh, what is this? A pep talk?

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:29.695
-Stop hiding. Seriously. Okay?

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:31.531
Stop being all meek and [bleep]
and running away.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:33.799
-What? You want me to run
through lightning

00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:34.901
with my [bleep] out?

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:38.070
-Yeah. Please. Exactly that.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.139
Run through lightning
with your [bleep] out.

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:41.674
[Bleep] it.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:45.044
-Please welcome back to
the show Paul Dano, everybody.

00:00:45.044 --> 00:00:47.347
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.794
Welcome back.

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:03.363
-How's it going?
-It's great.

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:06.166
It's great to have you back.
I really love this film.

00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:11.103
This is about the GameStop --
I don't want to say scandal.

00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:13.739
-Stock saga.
-The stock saga.

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:14.773
-Yeah.
-Exactly.

00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:16.342
And, yet,
this is the second time

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:17.510
we've had someone
from the movie on,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.511
and we keep showing
the clip of Pete Davidson

00:01:19.511 --> 00:01:21.214
talking about [bleep]

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:22.982
Which is
a very small part of this movie.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:26.719
-It's -- Well, it's a big small
part of the film, Seth.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.488
-It's a big small part.
You've said it correctly.

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:32.625
-If you're Pete Davidson,
it's easy to tell other people

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.560
to run around with their
[bleep] out, I guess.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.897
-Yeah. You you did play --
The actual person

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.965
you played in the movie,
Keith Gill --

00:01:39.965 --> 00:01:41.801
he was a runner in high school.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.936
This is a true thing.
-He was a runner.

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:45.605
In fact, I think he got
a scholarship running.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:49.342
I decided to start to run,
you know, to get into shape

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:50.676
and feel like a real actor.

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:53.045
And I started waking up
before my kids got up.

00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:56.549
I'd go to the track behind
the school that's closest to us.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.385
And then I get to work
to go film the scene,

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:03.256
and we are, indeed, filming on
a track that's behind a school.

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.090
But the track where
we're actually filming on

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.393
is behind a high school,
and it turns out the track

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.862
that I was running on
was behind an elementary school.

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:10.963
-Oh, gotcha.

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:12.632
-And they're
two different sizes.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.600
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.902
-So, I was -- You know,

00:02:16.902 --> 00:02:20.373
if I did like four laps
on the elementary-school track,

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:21.641
I felt pretty good about myself.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:23.042
-Yeah.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.911
-And doing one lap
on the high-school track,

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.846
I didn't feel so good
about myself, so...

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.881
-It was -- I will say,
through movie magic.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:30.483
I thought you were
a pretty believable runner.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:31.650
So well done.
-Thanks. Yeah.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:32.785
-I like, though,
that you showed up to set

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.253
and you're like,
"I can do four laps."

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:36.623
And then you're like, "I might
have gotten that wrong."

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.157
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:41.360
-Now, you know, I remember
this story as it was happening,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.962
but I will also admit
to not fully

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:45.264
comprehending the details of it.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:46.799
How well did you know
this story?

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.200
-I didn't know it well.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.102
I probably listened to, like,
the episode on "The Daily"

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:52.504
that was like, "This
GameStop thing is happening,"

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:55.074
and that was actually
one of the pleasures of

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.243
the script was going,
"You know, okay, wow.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:00.513
This is actually fascinating.
It's a real story."

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:01.914
It's a "subculture,"

00:03:01.914 --> 00:03:04.717
but it's like 8 million people
strong on Reddit

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.685
who sort of rose up
against Wall Street

00:03:06.685 --> 00:03:09.288
and actually took it
to The Man for a moment.

00:03:09.288 --> 00:03:11.291
You know, a lot of these firms
and these hedge funds,

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:12.525
now they have to hire people

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.927
just to track
what people are doing online,

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:16.262
people who they used
to make fun of

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.364
and think we're
shark bait for them.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:21.033
And now they actually
have to pay attention, so...

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:23.669
-We had the writers
on during the actors' strike,

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:25.671
because it's very hard
to promote a film like this

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:28.107
that has such a wonderful cast.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:31.010
Nick Offerman, Seth Rogen,
yourself, Pete Davidson,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.147
obviously, Shailene Woodley.

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:35.481
-Yeah.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:38.818
-But we had Lauren Schuker Blum
and Rebecca Angelo.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.186
They're former journalists.

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:42.054
Did it feel like, when you were
reading the script, like,

00:03:42.054 --> 00:03:43.389
"Oh, I can tell

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.592
that they're both great,
like, sort of scriptwriters,

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:47.993
but also have
a journalism background"?

00:03:47.993 --> 00:03:49.662
-Well, I don't know
that I knew that,

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:52.532
but it was really thorough

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.934
and thorough
in, like, a concise way,

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.368
which is not --
So, as you could tell

00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:58.171
the way I'm telling this story,
that's not my forte.

00:03:58.171 --> 00:04:02.075
Like, how do you sort of
make that information?

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:04.376
Also, how do you make that
information like action, right?

00:04:04.376 --> 00:04:08.247
So that it's super-fun to watch
and that you're also

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:10.516
sort of taking in
and learning something.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.986
And, again,
I enjoyed learning about it.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:18.290
I mean, I was not even on social
media before doing this film.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.426
And then I thought, "Well, okay,
I have to get in touch.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:23.529
Like, I have to be a person
who's in touch with the world."

00:04:23.529 --> 00:04:25.964
So I made these
social-media accounts.

00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:28.667
And, like, I watched --
I ended up watching these videos

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:31.371
that people made during
the GameStop phenomena

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:33.005
every morning
before I went to set.

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.073
People made songs.
They made these videos.

00:04:35.073 --> 00:04:37.877
They made these mash-ups.
And that, like, energized me.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:41.647
I had to take the energy of the
people and bring it to work.

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:44.316
-And it was -- Because it was
a real sense of community.

00:04:44.316 --> 00:04:46.252
I think that when we think
about the stock market,

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.854
we think about all --
it's all based on sort of greed

00:04:48.854 --> 00:04:50.522
and avarice and making money.

00:04:50.522 --> 00:04:52.691
But this GameStop story
was a group of people

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:55.394
that there was sort of
a collective sense of,

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:58.764
"We're doing something bigger
than just making cash."

00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:01.300
-Yeah. And this was also
during the pandemic,

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.037
and I think people needed
something to, like, believe in.

00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:05.271
They needed something
to get behind.

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:07.840
And one of the things
I loved about my guy,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.142
whose name is Keith Gill,
who went by Roaring Kitty,

00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:12.478
is just how honest he was.

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:15.882
And I feel like the stock market
is a lot of smoke and mirrors,

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:17.550
right?

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.153
And he was not that.
He was like, "No, this is me.

00:05:21.153 --> 00:05:24.624
You can literally watch me trade
for three hours online.

00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:27.593
Here's my spreadsheet.
Here's my account transparency.

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:28.894
Like, look."

00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:32.465
And that realness,
I think, meant a lot to people.

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:36.002
-So, you did some running.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:38.403
-You did some social accounts.
Did some social.

00:05:38.403 --> 00:05:40.606
-Can we assume now that
you're a giant stock trader?

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:42.508
-Just --
That's been my strike job.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:45.143
You know, three monitors
-Yeah.

00:05:45.143 --> 00:05:48.414
It's hard to picture you
without a headset, really.

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:51.050
-No, I'm actually terrified
of that.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.586
I hate gambling.
I hate losing money.

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:55.788
And if I was to get into that,
I am the type of person

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:58.758
who I have to get obsessed
and go, like, all out.

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:01.360
So I would stop
what my current job is

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.662
and I would have
to commit to that,

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:05.364
and that is not something
I want to commit to.

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:08.000
I'd rather go make a movie.
-Yeah.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.302
-Or if you commit to it,
it's, like, a real bummer

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:11.671
to have to go tell your family.

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:14.440
Like, "I know I've been doing
acting for a long time,

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.243
but I think I got a new itch."

00:06:19.611 --> 00:06:21.747
This was the other thing
about this movie,

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:22.815
and it's really impressive,

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:24.951
is how quickly
it was turned around,

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.486
because this is about
very recent history.

00:06:27.486 --> 00:06:29.522
And, you know,
you've made movies

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:30.823
based on things
that have happened in the past,

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:32.191
but I'm assuming
you've never made something

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.760
that happened
in such the recent past.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:35.994
-I don't think so.

00:06:35.994 --> 00:06:38.564
And the way information
moves nowadays,

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:42.467
just in the news and online,
you got to get in there.

00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:45.004
You got to jump on it.

00:06:45.004 --> 00:06:48.607
And, again, it's weird that --

00:06:48.607 --> 00:06:50.543
It's weird that there's so many
pieces of recent history

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:52.912
that mean so much
to so many people,

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:54.313
and, yet, I can come here
and just say,

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:57.283
"Oh, I just heard about it
on 'The Daily' and --"

00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:58.617
Yeah, but I think
there's, like --

00:06:58.617 --> 00:07:01.119
There was a real pleasure
in the sort of modern element

00:07:01.119 --> 00:07:02.755
of this for me,
as opposed to a period piece,

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:05.390
like "The Fabelmans"
or something,

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.626
where I think I just got to
bring, like, a lot of myself

00:07:07.626 --> 00:07:09.962
to work
and to be around guys like Pete

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:11.630
and just, like, be a dude.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.667
And the movie's funny.
And so I had a really good time.

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:17.436
-You did not reach out
to Keith Gill.

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:18.604
-No.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.773
-What was your reasoning
behind that?

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:23.876
-Well, okay, so,
it was the guy who I play.

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:26.512
Basically, Wall Street kind of
teamed up on him, ultimately.

00:07:26.512 --> 00:07:29.748
He got deposed by Congress
and he had to testify

00:07:29.748 --> 00:07:33.452
against a bunch of billionaires
with all their lawyers.

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:35.020
And this is just, you know,

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.791
sort of an
average-but-extraordinary guy.

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:42.962
It felt like he needed to step
away from what he was doing,

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.631
and it just felt like the vibe
he was putting out

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:48.467
was not a "Come stand outside
my house" vibe

00:07:48.467 --> 00:07:49.802
and track me down.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:52.238
It was a "I need
to step away for a moment

00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:53.673
because of what's happened."

00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:55.407
And I can't -- This happened,
again, during the pandemic,

00:07:55.407 --> 00:07:58.277
so the deposition, which you
can see it was on Zoom,

00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:01.213
but, I mean, this is a guy --
If I got called in

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:03.015
to testify in front of
the senators, you know,

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:05.050
and billionaires -- terrifying.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:09.121
So I just wanted
to respect his space.

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:12.825
-I think that was
the kind and correct choice.

00:08:12.825 --> 00:08:14.560
Last time you were here
was around the holidays,

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.863
and your daughter --
Not to bring up,

00:08:17.863 --> 00:08:19.398
you know, a painful memory.

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:21.734
Pink eye last time.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.669
Are you more hopeful
about these holidays?

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:25.170
-That's right.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.272
I came out and gave you a pound
and I was like, "Don't worry.

00:08:27.272 --> 00:08:29.742
I washed my hands."

00:08:29.742 --> 00:08:30.876
Yeah, no, no, no,

00:08:30.876 --> 00:08:33.078
I love the holidays.
I love Thanksgiving.

00:08:33.078 --> 00:08:34.113
I love this time of year
here in New York.

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:35.247
-Do you have
a big Thanksgiving?

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:37.149
-We do. We have my dad.

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:39.118
We have like 50 to 60 people
every year.

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:40.886
-50 to 60.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:42.587
And they're people
I only see once a year.

00:08:42.587 --> 00:08:43.956
And, sadly, the pandemic,

00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:45.491
we kind of had
a couple years off,

00:08:45.491 --> 00:08:47.125
which, again,
it's people I only see --

00:08:47.125 --> 00:08:50.128
It's basically a family reunion
every Thanksgiving.

00:08:50.128 --> 00:08:53.598
One of my cousins,
thankfully, has reorganized.

00:08:53.598 --> 00:08:55.801
And there's pictures
that predate my existence,

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:58.070
from the '70s,
of this Thanksgiving.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.306
So the the
Dano family Thanksgiving --

00:09:00.306 --> 00:09:01.774
it's a real thing.

00:09:01.774 --> 00:09:03.909
We take a photo together
every year.

00:09:03.909 --> 00:09:06.912
You can see everybody growing.
For me, it's really beautiful.

00:09:06.912 --> 00:09:08.380
So it's probably
my favorite holiday.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.416
-Where do you guys do it?
Where in the country?

00:09:10.416 --> 00:09:11.850
-In Pennsylvania.
-In Pennsylvania.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.721
And do you have to --
Is your role as one of the 60 --

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:17.189
do you have
to bring something?

00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:19.625
Do you have to cook some food?

00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:22.261
-You know, so, my --
I probably should.

00:09:22.261 --> 00:09:25.063
-Yeah. Because it seems.
like you really love it

00:09:25.063 --> 00:09:26.298
and it's very important to you,

00:09:26.298 --> 00:09:28.033
and, yet, you're just
kind of freeloading.

00:09:28.033 --> 00:09:32.104
-Because I grew up going,
I think I'm taking, you know,

00:09:32.104 --> 00:09:34.740
my young self
and thinking that my adults

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:35.941
are still going to
provide for me.

00:09:35.941 --> 00:09:39.211
I'm going bring
something really nice this year.

00:09:39.211 --> 00:09:43.081
-I did say to my --
I said to my wife,

00:09:43.081 --> 00:09:44.416
"You know, it's funny --
like, growing up,

00:09:44.416 --> 00:09:46.451
like, Thanksgiving was always
my favorite holiday."

00:09:46.451 --> 00:09:47.953
And she's like,
"That's because you were a kid

00:09:47.953 --> 00:09:49.555
and you didn't have to do
anything."

00:09:49.555 --> 00:09:50.756
-Right.

00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:52.958
-She's like, "Your memory
is, like, a false memory.

00:09:52.958 --> 00:09:54.927
Now you're an adult,
you have to get up off your ass.

00:09:54.927 --> 00:09:56.095
I'm sorry."

00:09:56.095 --> 00:09:59.498
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah.
I do dishes.

00:09:59.498 --> 00:10:02.667
-Oh, that's really sweet.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:02.667 --> 00:10:06.204
-Thanks. Yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:10:06.204 --> 00:10:08.340
-Now, I feel like we've --

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.976
When we've been here and we've
talked about your work,

00:10:10.976 --> 00:10:12.744
you know, "The Batman,"
"Escape from Dannemora,"

00:10:12.744 --> 00:10:14.747
which I loved so much,
your kids --

00:10:14.747 --> 00:10:17.449
have you done anything
yet your kids can watch?

00:10:17.449 --> 00:10:19.184
-Ah. No.

00:10:19.184 --> 00:10:22.121
I mean, even this one, you got
Pete running around naked.

00:10:22.121 --> 00:10:24.690
You know,
I can't show that to them.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.693
-Yeah.
-No. It would be fun to.

00:10:27.693 --> 00:10:29.861
That's, like, really where
I'm at in my life right now,

00:10:29.861 --> 00:10:31.797
especially this strike year
is, like --

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:33.398
you know, I got a 1-year-old
and a 5-year-old --

00:10:33.398 --> 00:10:34.767
is be with my kids.

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:36.869
It would be fun to do something
that they could watch.

00:10:36.869 --> 00:10:39.037
They don't know what we do,
even our 5-year-old,

00:10:39.037 --> 00:10:40.239
which I like.

00:10:40.239 --> 00:10:43.208
But it's funny that --
I don't know if it's just

00:10:43.208 --> 00:10:44.643
what kids do
or if they intuit it,

00:10:44.643 --> 00:10:47.680
but she said for her birthday,
which is, like, next summer,

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.050
but we're already planning it,
she wants to be a costume party.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.251
She wants to write a story

00:10:53.251 --> 00:10:55.588
and have everybody
act it out for the party.

00:10:55.588 --> 00:10:57.590
And I was like,
"Okay, is that just you?

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:00.726
Is that somehow, like --"
It's amazing, yeah.

00:11:00.726 --> 00:11:03.195
-What does she think you do?

00:11:03.195 --> 00:11:06.932
-I think she thinks I'm
somebody who's on a computer.

00:11:06.932 --> 00:11:08.801
-Okay.

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:10.135
So, she's like,
"What does your dad do?"

00:11:10.135 --> 00:11:11.503
She's like, "Computer."
-Computer.

00:11:11.503 --> 00:11:12.638
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Computer.

00:11:12.638 --> 00:11:15.474
-"He's very good at computer."
-Yeah.

00:11:15.474 --> 00:11:16.808
-Well, it's a wonderful --

00:11:16.808 --> 00:11:18.444
I hope one day, you make
something that they can watch

00:11:18.444 --> 00:11:21.313
or they'll just get old enough
and enjoy your current work.

00:11:21.313 --> 00:11:22.548
It's always such a pleasure
to see you.

00:11:22.548 --> 00:11:24.316
Thanks so much, Paul.
-Thanks. Yeah.

00:11:24.316 --> 00:11:25.550
-Paul Dano, everybody.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.252
"Dumb Money" is in theaters
and on demand now.

00:11:27.252 --> 00:11:28.587
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

